BEGIN;
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A knit fabric technologist compares Lycra/spandex draw ratio settings for a swimwear fabric versus a basic t-shirt fabric, both using the SAME base yarn and SAME knit structure. Swimwear typically uses a HIGHER elastane draw ratio than a basic t-shirt. What functional requirement justifies this difference, and what tradeoff does the higher draw ratio bring?', '["Draw ratio has no relationship to end-use functional requirements; swimwear and t-shirts should always use mathematically identical draw ratios regardless of their different functional needs","Swimwear needs significantly HIGHER stretch/recovery POWER (to maintain compression/fit during vigorous movement and repeated wet/dry cycles, and to resist water-logged sagging) — a higher draw ratio delivers this greater recovery force (as established earlier, higher draw ratio means more contraction force when the elastane relaxes after knitting), but this comes at the cost of a tighter, more compressive hand-feel and generally a lower dimensional ''give'' before reaching maximum stretch, which would feel uncomfortably restrictive for a relaxed-fit basic t-shirt application","Basic t-shirt fabric always uses a HIGHER elastane draw ratio than swimwear, the reverse of the actual typical relationship between these two product categories","Elastane draw ratio has zero relationship to stretch/recovery power, making this entire comparison scenario''s premise about a functional-requirement-driven difference technically incorrect"]'::jsonb, 'Swimwear needs significantly HIGHER stretch/recovery POWER (to maintain compression/fit during vigorous movement and repeated wet/dry cycles, and to resist water-logged sagging) — a higher draw ratio delivers this greater recovery force (as established earlier, higher draw ratio means more contraction force when the elastane relaxes after knitting), but this comes at the cost of a tighter, more compressive hand-feel and generally a lower dimensional ''give'' before reaching maximum stretch, which would feel uncomfortably restrictive for a relaxed-fit basic t-shirt application', 'This directly applies the earlier elastane-draw-ratio discussion to a real end-use comparison — swimwear''s functional requirements (maintaining compression/fit through vigorous movement, resisting water-logged sagging, repeated wet-dry cycling) genuinely demand more stretch/recovery POWER than a relaxed-fit basic t-shirt needs, and since draw ratio directly controls this recovery force (established earlier), swimwear justifiably uses a higher draw ratio — but this comes with the established tradeoff of a tighter, more compressive hand-feel, which would be genuinely uncomfortable/inappropriate for a basic t-shirt''s intended relaxed fit, illustrating that draw ratio selection is a deliberate, end-use-specific engineering decision, not a single universal ''more is better'' setting.', 3, '{"draw-ratio-enduse-comparison"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A knit technologist explains ''sinker loop'' versus ''needle loop'' as the two components of a single weft-knit stitch, and that their RELATIVE lengths (not just total loop length) affect fabric properties. What does an UNEVEN sinker-loop-to-needle-loop ratio (compared to a properly balanced structure) typically indicate about a specific knitting fault?', '["Sinker loop and needle loop length always have zero relationship to each other or to any knitting fault, making this comparison technically meaningless","An imbalance between sinker loop and needle loop length within the SAME stitch structure (beyond normal structural design variation between different stitch TYPES) can indicate inconsistent yarn feed tension DURING the specific moment of sinker action versus needle action within a single knitting cycle — since both loop components are formed from the same continuous yarn feed but at slightly different mechanical moments/tensions in the cycle, a chronic imbalance points to a timing or tension-control issue specifically affecting one part of the loop-formation cycle more than the other","Sinker loops and needle loops are always mathematically required to be EXACTLY equal in length for any knit structure to be considered valid, making any observed length difference automatically a fault regardless of stitch type","This distinction only applies to warp knitting, never to weft knitting, making the entire premise of comparing sinker and needle loop lengths in a weft-knit stitch technically inapplicable"]'::jsonb, 'An imbalance between sinker loop and needle loop length within the SAME stitch structure (beyond normal structural design variation between different stitch TYPES) can indicate inconsistent yarn feed tension DURING the specific moment of sinker action versus needle action within a single knitting cycle — since both loop components are formed from the same continuous yarn feed but at slightly different mechanical moments/tensions in the cycle, a chronic imbalance points to a timing or tension-control issue specifically affecting one part of the loop-formation cycle more than the other', 'This is an advanced, granular knitting-mechanics diagnostic insight — a single weft-knit stitch is actually composed of two distinct geometric components (the needle loop, formed around the needle, and the sinker loop, the connecting segment controlled by the sinker between adjacent needles), and while their RELATIVE proportions naturally differ somewhat between different stitch types by design, a CHRONIC imbalance within what should be a consistent stitch structure suggests the yarn feed tension is being applied inconsistently between the specific moments when the needle forms its loop portion versus when the sinker controls its portion of the SAME knitting cycle — a subtle, mechanically-specific diagnostic clue pointing toward sinker-needle TIMING synchronization (connecting to the earlier sinker-timing discussion) rather than an overall yarn-tension problem.', 3, '{"sinker-needle-loop-balance"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A garment technologist compares a fully-fashioned knit sweater panel (shaped directly during knitting, needles progressively deactivated at the armhole) against a cut-and-sewn sweater panel (knitted as a larger rectangle, then armhole shape cut and overlocked). Beyond the earlier-discussed edge-stability difference, what SPECIFIC fabric CONSUMPTION difference exists between these two approaches for the SAME finished garment shape?', '["Fully-fashioned and cut-and-sew approaches always consume mathematically identical amounts of yarn/fabric for the same finished shape, making any consumption comparison technically pointless","Fully-fashioned knitting produces ONLY the fabric actually needed for the finished shaped panel (needles are deactivated exactly where the shape requires, directly analogous to the earlier woven marker-efficiency concept but achieved through the KNITTING process itself rather than a cutting layout), while cut-and-sew knits a larger rectangular piece and then DISCARDS the cut-away excess material as waste — meaning fully-fashioned technology inherently achieves something like 100% marker efficiency for that specific panel, while cut-and-sew knitting always generates genuine fabric waste proportional to how much material falls outside the final cut shape","Cut-and-sew knitting always uses LESS total yarn than fully-fashioned knitting for the identical finished shape, the reverse of the actual relationship between these two production approaches","Fabric consumption has no relationship to whether a knit panel is fully-fashioned or cut-and-sew, making this entire comparison technically irrelevant to garment production economics"]'::jsonb, 'Fully-fashioned knitting produces ONLY the fabric actually needed for the finished shaped panel (needles are deactivated exactly where the shape requires, directly analogous to the earlier woven marker-efficiency concept but achieved through the KNITTING process itself rather than a cutting layout), while cut-and-sew knits a larger rectangular piece and then DISCARDS the cut-away excess material as waste — meaning fully-fashioned technology inherently achieves something like 100% marker efficiency for that specific panel, while cut-and-sew knitting always generates genuine fabric waste proportional to how much material falls outside the final cut shape', 'This connects the earlier fully-fashioned-vs-cut-sew EDGE discussion with a separate, additional dimension: material CONSUMPTION/waste, directly paralleling the earlier woven marker-efficiency cost discussion — since fully-fashioned knitting directly knits ONLY the shape needed (via progressive needle deactivation), it inherently avoids the cutting waste that cut-and-sew knitting generates (knitting a larger rectangle, then cutting away and discarding the excess outside the final pattern shape) — this material-efficiency advantage (alongside the earlier-established edge-quality advantage) is a genuine, additional economic reason fully-fashioned technology is favored for higher-value knitwear despite its greater machine capability/cost requirements.', 3, '{"fully-fashioned-material-efficiency"}'::text[]);
COMMIT;
